          ],
          "content": "O"
        },
        {
          "type": "header",
          "bbox": [
            0.468,
            0.117,
            0.776,
            0.14
          ],
          "content": "Revise, Reflect, Refine"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.236,
            0.184,
            0.889,
            0.236
          ],
          "content": "1. Choose the **correct** options and explain the reason for the correct and incorrect options in the context of Ernest Rutherford’s gold foil experiment:"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.27,
            0.239,
            0.888,
            0.272
          ],
          "content": "(i) The experiment clearly showed the existence of neutrons in the nucleus."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.266,
            0.272,
            0.888,
            0.306
          ],
          "content": "(ii) The results disproved the plum pudding model and led to the idea of a nucleus at the centre of the atom."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.261,
            0.306,
            0.889,
            0.356
          ],
          "content": "(iii) The large deflection of a few alpha particles indicated that most of the mass of the atom and positive charge are packed into a tiny centre."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.262,
            0.357,
            0.888,
            0.391
          ],
          "content": "(iv) The way alpha particles were deflected showed that electrons move around the nucleus."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.236,
            0.394,
            0.889,
            0.429
          ],
          "content": "2. Which of the following statements are **correct** or **incorrect** according to the Bohr’s atomic model? Give a reason for each statement."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.27,
            0.431,
            0.888,
            0.465
          ],
          "content": "(i) Electrons lose energy while moving in fixed orbits and slowly fall into the nucleus."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.266,
            0.465,
            0.888,
            0.499
          ],
          "content": "(ii) Electrons can exist anywhere around the nucleus with no fixed energy."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.261,
            0.499,
            0.888,
            0.533
          ],
          "content": "(iii) Electrons revolve around the nucleus in orbits of fixed energy without losing energy."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.262,
            0.533,
            0.888,
            0.567
          ],
          "content": "(iv) Electrons can be found between energy levels as they move around the nucleus."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.236,
            0.569,
            0.888,
            0.605
          ],
          "content": "3. The composition of the nuclei of three atomic species X, Y, and Z are given as follows."
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "bbox": [
            0.259,
            0.606,
            0.884,
            0.707
          ],
          "content": "|  | X | Y | Z |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Number of protons | 18 | 17 | 17 |\n| Number of neutrons | 19 | 18 | 20 |",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [
            {
              "bbox": [
                0.265,
                0.717,
                0.654,
                0.736
              ],
              "content": "Explain the relation between the following:"
            }
          ],
          "image_source": "images/page_18_table_13.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.272,
            0.742,
            0.38,
            0.759
          ],
          "content": "(i) Y and Z"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.267,
            0.759,
            0.381,
            0.776
          ],
          "content": "(ii) Z and X"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.236,
            0.779,
            0.889,
            0.85
          ],
          "content": "4. What conclusion did Rutherford draw about the position and characteristics of the atom’s positively charged part based on the few alpha particles that bounced back or were deflected at large angles in the gold foil experiment?"
        },
